The problem: find eventual safe states. Classic approach: reverse graph topological sort (Kahn's algorithm) or DFS with coloring.

Approach 1: DFS with colors - color 0 = unvisited, 1 = visiting (in stack), 2 = safe. If during DFS we hit a node in "visiting" state, it's part of a cycle → unsafe. Otherwise mark safe.

Approach 2: Reverse graph + outdegree. Compute outdegree of each node. Reverse edges. Start with terminal nodes (outdegree 0), process queue, decrement outdegree of predecessors; when outdegree becomes 0, node is safe.

Let me write the reverse topological sort approach:

```javascript
var eventualSafeNodes = function(graph) {
    const n = graph.length;
    const outdegree = new Array(n).fill(0);
    const reverseAdj = Array.from({length: n}, () => []);
    for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        outdegree[i] = graph[i].length;
        for (const j of graph[i]) {
            reverseAdj[j].push(i);
        }
    }
    const queue = [];
    for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        if (outdegree[i] === 0) queue.push(i);
    }
    const safe = new Array(n).fill(false);
    while (queue.length) {
        const node = queue.shift(); // better use index pointer for performance
        safe[node] = true;
        for (const prev of reverseAdj[node]) {
            if (--outdegree[prev] === 0) queue.push(prev);
        }
    }
    const result = [];
    for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) if (safe[i]) result.push(i);
    return result;
};
```

Use index pointer instead of shift for O(1) dequeue.

Alternatively DFS with memoization:

```javascript
