The problem is the classic Longest Increasing Subsequence. The follow-up asks for O(n log n), so I'll use the patience sorting approach with binary search.

The algorithm:
- Maintain a `tails` array where `tails[i]` is the smallest tail value of any increasing subsequence of length `i+1`.
- For each number in nums, use binary search to find the leftmost position in `tails` where the value is >= current number (lower bound), and replace it. If no such position exists (number is greater than all tails), append it.
- The length of `tails` at the end is the answer.

Binary search: use lower bound (first index where tails[idx] >= x). Since we want strictly increasing, if x equals an existing tail, we replace it (not extend), which lower bound handles correctly.

Let me write the code:

```javascript
/**
 * @param {number[]} nums
 * @return {number}
 */
var lengthOfLIS = function(nums) {
    const tails = [];
    for (const num of nums) {
        let lo = 0, hi = tails.length;
        // find lower bound: first index with tails[idx] >= num
        while (lo < hi) {
            const mid = (lo + hi) >> 1;
            if (tails[mid] < num) {
                lo = mid + 1;
            } else {
                hi = mid;
            }
        }
        if (lo === tails.length) {
            tails.push(num);
        } else {
            tails[lo] = num;
        }
    }
    return tails.length;
};
```

